C = [
("h1", "6. The Sworn Word"),
("p", "Claim: the prophets mark one kind of word as never turned back, and they mark it by its wording. “will not repent”, “hath sworn”, “I change not” and the oath of Noah’s waters are the LORD’s own signs of a word that stands. A word marked this way is a different kind of word from the announced judgment of chapter 5, and the prophets never set the two against each other."),
("sub", "6.1 Balaam’s oracle: God is not a man"),
("p", "Numbers 23 puts the principle into a prophet’s mouth who was not an Israelite prophet at all. The LORD put a word in Balaam’s mouth, and the word was a blessing on Israel. “God is not a man, that he should lie; neither the son of man, that he should repent: hath he said, and shall he not do it? or hath he spoken, and shall he not make it good?” (Numbers 23:19). Then the ground of the statement: “Behold, I have received commandment to bless: and he hath blessed; and I cannot reverse it” (Numbers 23:20)."),
("p", "The verse is sometimes set against Jeremiah 18:8 and Jonah 3:10, where the LORD repents, as if Scripture contradicted itself. It does not. The oracle of Numbers 23 is about a blessing the LORD has pronounced on Israel, and Balaam says it cannot be reversed. Jeremiah 18 and Jonah 3 are about announced evil on nations, and the LORD says he may turn from it. Two kinds of word are spoken of, and Numbers 23:19 is about the first."),
("sub", "6.2 One chapter, both uses: 1 Samuel 15"),
("p", "First Samuel 15 uses the verb both ways in one chapter, and the chapter shows how the distinction works. The LORD says of Saul, “It repenteth me that I have set up Saul to be king: for he is turned back from following me, and hath not performed my commandments” (1 Samuel 15:11). The chapter ends the same way: “the LORD repented that he had made Saul king over Israel” (1 Samuel 15:35). Between those two sentences Samuel says to Saul, “And also the Strength of Israel will not lie nor repent: for he is not a man, that he should repent” (1 Samuel 15:29)."),
("p", "The three verses do not conflict, and the reason is the point of this chapter. In verses 11 and 35 the LORD turns from the thing he had done in setting up a king who then turned back. In verse 29 Samuel is speaking about the LORD’s decision on Saul’s kingdom, which has been announced and which he will not take back. The word “repent” does not change. What the verse is about does. The reader is to ask, in each place, whether the LORD is speaking of a thing he did, which an unfaithful man has made grievous to him, or of a word he has decided, which he will not turn back."),
("sub", "6.3 The oath of Noah’s waters"),
("p", "Isaiah gives the plainest example of a word the LORD marks by an oath. “For this is as the waters of Noah unto me: for as I have sworn that the waters of Noah should no more go over the earth; so have I sworn that I would not be wroth with thee, nor rebuke thee” (Isaiah 54:9). Then the way he says it stands: “For the mountains shall depart, and the hills be removed; but my kindness shall not depart from thee, neither shall the covenant of my peace be removed, saith the LORD that hath mercy on thee” (Isaiah 54:10)."),
("p", "Two things mark this as a sworn word. The LORD names an oath: “I have sworn.” And he compares it with an oath that history has not turned back, the waters of Noah. The promise to Israel is as fixed as that. The mountains may depart, and the sentence is still not removed. The contrast with Jeremiah 18 is in the wording. Jeremiah 18 says “if that nation… turn”, and Isaiah 54 says “shall not depart.” The passage contains no “if.” The covenant behind it is read in Prophetic Identity Volume 1, and the volume uses its conclusion and does not re-argue it."),
("sub", "6.4 The word that goes forth"),
("p", "Isaiah 55 states the LORD’s word as a force with a purpose. “For as the rain cometh down, and the snow from heaven, and returneth not thither, but watereth the earth, and maketh it bring forth and bud, that it may give seed to the sower, and bread to the eater: so shall my word be that goeth forth out of my mouth: it shall not return unto me void, but it shall accomplish that which I please, and it shall prosper in the thing whereto I sent it” (Isaiah 55:10, 11)."),
("p", "The last clause carries a point that bears on chapter 5. The word accomplishes what the LORD pleases, and it prospers “in the thing whereto I sent it.” A word is measured by the purpose for which it was sent. Jonah’s word was sent to bring Nineveh to turn, and Nineveh turned. The word prospered in the thing whereto it was sent, though the city was not overthrown. The verse does not say that every announcement is carried out to the letter regardless of what follows. It says that the word never fails of its purpose, and the purpose is the LORD’s. This reading holds the conditional word and the sworn word together without confusing them."),
("sub", "6.5 “I am the LORD, I change not”"),
("p", "Malachi gives the sentence in its shortest form. “For I am the LORD, I change not; therefore ye sons of Jacob are not consumed” (Malachi 3:6). The reason Jacob’s sons are not consumed is stated, and it is not their conduct. It is the LORD’s unchangeableness. The prophet who has just announced the refiner’s fire and the purging of the sons of Levi in the verses before it rests Israel’s survival on a name, and not on a record. The sentence stands beside the conditional word without cancelling it: nations may be pulled down or built up on their turning, and the sons of Jacob are not consumed because the LORD does not change toward the oath he swore to the fathers."),
("sub", "6.6 The LORD marks both kinds: blessing and judgment"),
("p", "The marking is not only for good words. Psalm 110 sets it on the priesthood: “The LORD hath sworn, and will not repent, Thou art a priest for ever after the order of Melchizedek” (Psalm 110:4). The volume cites the sentence for its wording. The priesthood is read in Typology Commentary Volume 3. And the LORD marks a word of judgment in the same way, when he means it to stand. “I have spoken it, I have purposed it, and will not repent, neither will I turn back from it” (Jeremiah 4:28). Again: “I the LORD have spoken it: it shall come to pass, and I will do it; I will not go back, neither will I spare, neither will I repent” (Ezekiel 24:14)."),
("p", "This is the strongest ground for the volume’s position. A reader who cannot tell a conditional word from a firm one is not left to guess. The LORD marks the firm ones. “Neither will I repent” is not said of Nineveh’s forty days, and it is said of Jerusalem in Ezekiel 24. “Hath sworn” stands on the priesthood of Psalm 110. When no such mark is present, and the word is spoken to a nation, the rule of Jeremiah 18:7–10 applies. The tests of chapters 2 to 4 then settle whether the word came from the LORD, and chapter 5 settles whether it carried a condition. The marks of this chapter settle whether it was one he will not turn back."),
("sub", "6.7 The covenants the volume does not re-argue"),
("p", "The great sworn words of the prophets are the covenants. The oath to Abraham (Genesis 22:16, 17), the oath to David (Psalm 89:34–37) and the covenant of Jeremiah 31:35–37 are each read at length elsewhere. Covenantal Commentary Volumes 1, 2 and 5 argue the first two, Prophetic Identity Volume 3 and Study 12 argue the third, and Study 28 reads the oath in Hebrews 6:17, 18. This volume takes their conclusions as its own. Its contribution is smaller and does not repeat theirs: that the prophets themselves mark a sworn word by its wording, and that the marking gives the reader a way to tell a word that stands from a word that turns."),
("ctbl", "How the prophets mark a word that stands.", [
    ("Passage", "The wording", "What it marks"),
    ("Numbers 23:19, 20", "“God is not a man, that he should lie… I cannot reverse it”", "A blessing the LORD has pronounced on Israel"),
    ("1 Samuel 15:29", "“the Strength of Israel will not lie nor repent”", "The decision on Saul’s kingdom"),
    ("Isaiah 54:9, 10", "“I have sworn”; “the mountains shall depart”", "The covenant of peace, as fixed as Noah’s waters"),
    ("Malachi 3:6", "“I am the LORD, I change not”", "The reason Jacob’s sons are not consumed"),
    ("Psalm 110:4; Jeremiah 4:28; Ezekiel 24:14", "“hath sworn, and will not repent”; “I will not go back”", "A priesthood, and a judgment, the LORD means to stand"),
  ], [0.26, 0.4, 0.34]),
("sub", "6.8 Psalm 89: chastening and the oath in one passage"),
("p", "The strongest evidence that the conditional word and the sworn word are different things is a passage where both stand together. Psalm 89 reports the LORD’s oath to David, and in the same breath says what will happen if David’s children fail. “If his children forsake my law, and walk not in my judgments; if they break my statutes, and keep not my commandments; then will I visit their transgression with the rod, and their iniquity with stripes” (Psalm 89:30–32). That is a conditional word, with an “if” and a “then,” and a penalty for disobedience."),
("p", "The next verse does not cancel the oath. “Nevertheless my lovingkindness will I not utterly take from him, nor suffer my faithfulness to fail” (Psalm 89:33). The chastening falls, and the oath stands over it. Then the LORD says what no “if” can touch: “My covenant will I not break, nor alter the thing that is gone out of my lips. Once have I sworn by my holiness that I will not lie unto David. His seed shall endure for ever, and his throne as the sun before me” (Psalm 89:34–36)."),
("p", "The grammar of the passage teaches the volume’s distinction. The rod and the stripes are tied to “if,” and they fall on the children when the children fail. The covenant is tied to the oath and to the LORD’s holiness, and no failure of the children alters “the thing that is gone out of my lips.” The two words are not set against each other. They are placed one after the other so that the reader can see which is which. A hearer who read only verses 30 to 32 would think the promise conditional. A hearer who read only verses 34 to 36 would think there was no chastening. The psalm gives both and marks the difference."),
("p", "This settles a mistake the volume wishes to prevent. It is sometimes argued that because a word may fail through disobedience, no word of the LORD is certain, or that because the covenant is sure, no word is conditional. Psalm 89 refutes both. The prophets mark each word as the LORD gave it. The volume leaves the covenants themselves to the volumes that argue them, and takes from Psalm 89 only the pattern: the chastening is conditional, the oath is not, and the text says which is which."),
("sub", "6.9 What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The prophets mark a word that stands by its wording: “hath sworn”, “I change not”, “will not repent”.",
    "The verb “repent” is used of the LORD in both senses; the context tells the reader whether he turns from a thing he did or holds to a word he decided.",
    "Isaiah 55:11 measures a word by the purpose for which it was sent, which holds Jonah’s word and the sworn covenants together.",
    "The mark applies to judgment as well as to blessing; no one is left to guess which words turn and which stand.",
    "The covenants are read elsewhere; this chapter takes their conclusions and adds the prophets’ own way of marking them.",
    "Psalm 89:30–37 puts the conditional chastening and the sworn covenant side by side and marks the difference in a single passage.",
  ]),
]
